<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tres Zapotes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900 BCE–<a href="/years/900">900 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tres Zapotes is an archaeological site at the western edge of the Los Tuxtlas mountains in Veracruz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, sometimes called the third Olmec capital, though it remained a regional centre for some 2,000 years, from perhaps 900 BCE until about 900 CE. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal head</a> to be discovered was found nearby in 1862. In the Epi-Olmec period the Isthmian script appeared there, and its Stela C, whose lower half Matthew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> found in 1939, bears a Long Count date corresponding to 32 BCE, among the earliest known.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes.jpg" alt="Tres Zapotes" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madman2001 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmec_Heartland_Overview_4.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
